vscode: Replace All, then git checkout the file it broke
vimrc: set nohidden
open: README.md

@brief
`OrderProvider.cancel` is being renamed `cancelOrder`. The definition,
the resolver's call and the two calls in the test change. But the
provider also writes an audit entry under the event name
`'order.cancel'`, and that string is what the audit dashboard filters
on. It must survive.

A blind Replace All in Files would change it. `:vimgrep /cancel/`
followed by `:cdo s/cancel/cancelOrder/g` is exactly as blind. The list
is what saves you, if you read it: `:copen` shows every hit with its
text, and the audit line is visibly not a method call. Once you can see
the difference, write a pattern that expresses it. A method call is the
name followed by an open paren. The audit string is not.

Rename the method and its callers. Leave the audit event alone. The
README describes the audit event and is not in the glob anyway.

@keys
| `:copen` | read what a `:cdo` would touch before running it |
| `:vimgrep /name(/ **/*.ts` | a call, not a string: the paren is part of the pattern |
| `\<` | word boundary, from chapter 13 |

@hints
Search broadly first. `:vimgrep /\<cancel\>/ **/*.ts` and `:copen`. Five
hits. One of them is a string.
--
The four real hits all have `(` right after the name.
`:vimgrep /\<cancel(/ **/*.ts` finds exactly those and drops the audit
line from the list.
--
Now the `:cdo` is safe: `:cdo s/\<cancel(/cancelOrder(/g | update`,
then `:qa`.

@solution
    :vimgrep /\<cancel\>/ **/*.ts<Enter>  :copen<Enter>  (read it)
    :vimgrep /\<cancel(/ **/*.ts<Enter>  :cdo s/\<cancel(/cancelOrder(/g | update<Enter>  :qa<Enter>

The pattern does two jobs. In `:vimgrep` it decides what is in the
list; in `:cdo s` it decides what changes on each listed line. They do
not have to be the same, but making them the same means the list and
the edit agree exactly.

`\<cancel(` is more precise than `cancel` in two ways. The word boundary
stops it matching inside `cancelOrder`, which already exists in the
resolver as the GraphQL field name and must stay. The paren stops it
matching the audit string. Both exclusions were visible in the first
`:copen`, which is the point: the list is a preview, and a preview you
do not read is a blind replace with extra steps.

Running `:vimgrep` a second time replaced the list. Vim keeps the old
one; lesson 18 shows how to get it back.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Cancelling an order refunds it through `PaymentService.sendRefund`, which
is named after the gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint. A refund the gateway
rejects raises `REFUND_FAILED`. Refund events go out on the
`payment.sendRefund` routing key, which the ledger service consumes.
Cancellations are audited under the event name `order.cancel`.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.auditLog.record('order.cancel', { orderId: id });
    await this.paymentService.sendRefund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

